  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Infrastructure — DNS Server", "technique": "T1584.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:02:11 CEST, Wazuh custom rule 92600 fired: a DNS A-record answer returned by the internal/ISP recursive resolver 10.108.57.1 for a well-known public domain did NOT match the answer returned by trusted public resolvers 8.8.8.8 and 1.1.1.1 for the same name. The poisoned answers for login.microsoftonline.com and update.microsoft.com pointed at 198.18.0.60 and 198.18.0.61 — addresses inside the IANA RFC5737/benchmark test range (198.18.0.0/15) which must never be a legitimate answer for a public domain. This is the canonical fingerprint of an upstream DNS-server compromise / ISP-level resolver hijack: the resolver itself is returning attacker-chosen addresses.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92600 'DNS response IP mismatch for known-good domain' correlated data.dns.rrname for a public domain with data.dns.rrtype=A and a data.dns.rdata value (198.18.0.60/61) that is both (a) inside RFC5737 test space and (b) divergent from the trusted-resolver baseline. MITRE T1584.002 (Compromise Infrastructure: DNS Server) attached automatically.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Infrastructure — DNS Server", "technique": "T1584.002", "what_happened": "At 09:06:33 CEST, internal host 10.108.57.42 followed the poisoned answer and opened TCP/443 sessions to 198.18.0.60. Suricata fired ET DNS signature 2014384 ('DNS answer from RFC1918/RFC5737 address for a public name') and the host's TLS client-hello carried SNI login.microsoftonline.com toward 198.18.0.60. The cert presented by the rogue endpoint did not chain to a trusted CA for that SNI — classic credential-harvest redirect: legitimate domain name, attacker-controlled landing IP.", "chain_reason": "Suricata data.alert.signature 'ET DNS Reply Sinkhole/RFC5737' (sig 2014384) with data.dstip=198.18.0.60, plus a Suricata TLS event where data.tls.sni=login.microsoftonline.com but data.dstip is in 198.18.0.0/15. The SNI-vs-destination-IP contradiction is the redirect proof.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:06:33+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Infrastructure — DNS Server", "technique": "T1584.002", "what_happened": "At 09:12:50 CEST, Wazuh rule 92601 fired: the poisoned A records for public domains were served by 10.108.57.1 with a TTL of 30 seconds — well under the 60s fast-flux threshold and far below the multi-hour TTLs these CDN-backed domains normally carry. Shortly after, rule 92602 fired a DNSSEC validation failure for microsoft.com via the same resolver. Suricata also fired ET DNS fast-flux signature 2017919. Low TTL + DNSSEC SERVFAIL + RFC5737 rdata together confirm the resolver is actively hijacking, not merely stale-cached.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92601 'DNS TTL below 60s for public domain' on data.dns.ttl=30 + rule 92602 'DNSSEC validation failure' + Suricata sig 2017919 'ET DNS suspicious fast-flux response', all with data.srcip=10.108.57.1 as the answering resolver. The three independent signals corroborate an active hijack.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:12:50+0200"}
